.TH KOSH 5 "2026-10-07" "kosh" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
kosh.conf \- Koshka settings files and the KOSHCONF encoding
.SH DESCRIPTION
Koshka settings live in koshconf files. They hold option values, not
shell commands. A shell loads them before any shell startup file, whether or
not it is interactive. The following do not read a settings file or apply
KOSHCONF: a shell started with
.B \-Q
or
.BR \-\-no\-config ,
a lint, format, or language server run, rescue mode, a shell whose real and
effective ids differ, a fresh evaluator receiving its state from its
parent, and a binary started as a koshkit utility or as
.BR koshkit .
Privileged mode,
.BR \-p ,
loads the files but ignores KOSHCONF.
.PP
The configured mood also applies to
.B kosh \-c
and to a script without a shebang, so a file that sets
.B mood=bash
runs such code under the bash grammar. Where the grammar matters, select the
mood with
.B \-\-mood
or a shebang.
The
.B koshconf
builtin in
.BR kosh (1)
loads, changes, and writes these settings.
.PP
Startup shell code belongs in the startup files listed under FILES. The
.B kosh
mood sources none unless init_moods selects some. See STARTUP MOODS.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR /etc/kosh.conf " or " %ProgramData%\kosh\kosh.conf
The system settings file, read first. It is read only when nobody else could
have written it. On POSIX the open file and
its directory must be owned by root and must not be writable by their group or
by others. The check runs on the descriptor then read, so the file cannot be
swapped in between. On Windows the ProgramData directory comes from the
system's known folder, not from an environment variable, and the file and its
.I kosh
directory must be owned by SYSTEM, Administrators, or TrustedInstaller. No
access control entry may let another account write, append, delete,
change the permissions of, or take ownership of the file. For the directory,
no entry may let another account delete its entries, delete or rename it,
change its permissions, or take ownership of it. Adding files and
subdirectories, granted to every user by the default ProgramData permissions,
is allowed on the directory, because replacing an existing file needs one of
the rights above and a file added by another account fails the owner check. An entry applying only to children, such as the usual CREATOR
OWNER entry, is not counted. A file failing the check is skipped
with a warning giving the reason and, on POSIX, suggesting the
.B chown root
or
.B chmod go\-w
command that would make the file trusted.
.TP
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/kosh.conf " or " ~/.config/kosh/kosh.conf
The user settings file, read second. XDG_CONFIG_HOME is used when it is set to
an absolute path.
.BR "koshconf create" ,
.BR "koshconf set \-\-persist" ,
and
.B koshconf save \-\-persist
write this file. A symbolic link, as left by a dotfile manager
such as stow, is kept and its final target is written instead. The
new text goes to a temporary file in the target's directory, which is flushed
and renamed over the target, so a reader sees either the old or the new
file. The directory is created when missing. The written file keeps the
permission bits of the file it replaces, and a new file is created with mode
0644 less the file creation mask. Each write holds an advisory lock on the
target's directory while loading and replacing the file, so concurrent
.B koshconf set \-\-persist
commands that change different options all take effect. On Windows the lock is
a hidden
.I .kosh\-flock.lock
file in that directory, and only the owner write bit of the mode is kept.
.TP
.I /etc/profile
The system login file of the
.B bash
and
.B sh
moods.
.TP
.I ~/.profile
The user POSIX login file and the last Bash login candidate.
.TP
.I ~/.bash_profile
The first user Bash login candidate.
.TP
.I ~/.bash_login
The second user Bash login candidate.
.TP
.IR /etc/bash/bashrc " or " /etc/bash.bashrc
The first readable path is the system rc file of an interactive non-login Bash mood.
.TP
.I ~/.bashrc
The default user rc file of an interactive non-login Bash mood.
.TP
.I /usr/share/bash-completion/bash_completion
Bash programmable completion, sourced after the Bash startup chain sources an rc
file. It is skipped when completion is disabled, when a default completion
specification exists, and when BASH_COMPLETION_VERSINFO is already set.
.PP
The
.B \-\-rcfile
and
.B \-\-init\-file
options and the ENV and BASH_ENV variables may select another startup
file in the
.B bash
and
.B sh
moods, as detailed in
.BR kosh (1).
.SH SYNTAX
A settings file is a sequence of lines ended by LF or CRLF. A UTF-8 byte
order mark at the start of the file is skipped. A line that is empty, holds only
blanks, or starts with a number sign after optional blanks is ignored.
.PP
Every other line has the form
.IR name = value .
Blanks around the name and the value are ignored, so
.B "name = on"
is the same as
.BR name=on .
A value starting with a double or a single quote ends at the next quote of
the same kind, and only blanks may follow it. Quote a value whose blanks
at either end must be kept.
Otherwise the value is everything between the blanks after the equals sign and
the blanks at the end of the line. A value cannot span lines.
.PP
A boolean is written as on or off and is also read as true, false, 1, or 0. An
enumeration is written by name. A string is taken as written and must be valid
UTF-8 without a NUL byte. editor.history.max_entries is a decimal integer from
0 to 2147483647. init_moods is a comma-separated list of moods accepted by
.BR \-L ,
such as kosh, sh, bash, and bash-posix. An empty name is skipped.
.B koshconf
quotes a written value starting with a quote or starting or ending with a blank.
A value containing a newline cannot be written to a file, nor can a value
containing both quote characters if it needs quoting.
.B koshconf set \-\-persist
rejects either value before changing the session.
.B koshconf list
prints one name=value line for each option, without a class column, and shows
such a value, and any value with a control byte or invalid UTF-8, in the shell
.B $\(aq...\(aq
form.
.PP
A file may set every option except those fixed by how the shell started,
such as bash.interpreter.login_shell, bash.interpreter.restricted_shell, bash.interpreter.privileged_mode,
posix.interpreter.parse_without_executing, and bash.interpreter.exit_after_one_command, and those
following the session, posix.interpreter.job_control, bash.editor.history.records_commands,
bash.editor.history.bang_expansion, and bash.interpreter.posixly_correct. The OPTION REGISTRY
section of
.BR kosh (1)
lists them.
.PP
.B A semantic option in a file changes every shell that loads it, scripts included.
For example, posix.interpreter.exit_on_command_failure=on makes every script exit on its
first failing command, and bash.interpreter.glob_includes_dotfiles=on changes what every
glob matches. Put such a setting in a file only when every script should use it. Otherwise set
it for a session with
.BR set ,
.BR shopt ,
or
.BR "koshconf set" .
.PP
The mood is applied first, wherever it appears in the file, so the other lines
are read under it. In the
.B kosh
mood, posix.interpreter.unset_variable_is_error, posix.interpreter.pipeline_fails_on_any_stage,
bash.interpreter.glob_no_match_is_error, and interpreter.arithmetic_uses_big_numbers stay
on and bash.interpreter.glob_no_match_expands_to_nothing stays off. A line
setting another value is skipped with a warning giving the file, line, option,
and
.BR "koshconf set mood bash" .
An option set on the command line, such as
.BR "\-u" ", " "\-e" ", " "+e" ", " "\-o pipefail" ", " "+O extglob" ", " "\-T" ", " "\-\-no\-traces" ", or " "\-L" ,
keeps the command-line value, so
.B kosh +e script
runs without errexit whatever a file says. Likewise, a KOSH_HISTORY_FILE
or KOSH_HISTORY_SIZE variable inherited from the environment keeps its value
over editor.history.file_path or editor.history.max_entries. A later line replaces an earlier one, and the
user file and KOSHCONF replace the system file.
.PP
The canonical name of an option inherited from POSIX
.B set \-o
starts with posix, and one inherited from any other
Bash
.B set \-o
or
.B shopt
option starts with bash, as in
posix.interpreter.exit_on_command_failure for errexit and
bash.interpreter.glob_extended_patterns for extglob. A name with neither
prefix belongs to kosh alone.
.PP
Every option with a Bash spelling is also accepted under an alias. The
alias is posix.legacy.NAME when POSIX
.B set \-o
accepts NAME, and otherwise bash.legacy.NAME when
.B set \-o
or
.B shopt
accepts NAME, as in bash.legacy.extglob and bash.legacy.posix. An alias resolves at lookup to
the same option entry as the canonical name, with the same id and storage, so
setting either spelling changes the value shown by the other,
.BR "set \-o" ,
.BR shopt ,
.BR $\- ,
and SHELLOPTS. A kosh.conf
line, koshconf set, koshconf get, and every other option-name
operand accept the alias.
.B koshconf create
and
.BR "koshconf list" ,
error messages, and KOSHCONF use only the canonical name, except that
.B koshconf list \-\-all
prints the alias of each option on a line after the
canonical line, with the same value.
.PP
.B koshconf create
writes every option a file may hold, with a blank line between topics, each
after a
.RI # " help"
comment that also identifies its
.B set \-o
or
.B shopt
spelling. A string option without a default, such as editor.history.file_path,
is written commented out. The
.B bash
preset also writes bash.interpreter.aliases_expand commented out, because bash expands
aliases only in an interactive shell and a written value would apply to both. The
.B kosh
preset writes the values held by the
.B kosh
mood, so reading it back never warns.
.SH STARTUP MOODS
init_moods lists the moods whose startup files an interactive or login
shell sources, in order, each file parsed under the grammar of its mood.
It is empty by default. An empty value sources the files of the session mood,
so it sources no files in the
.B kosh
mood. It is the configured form of
.BR \-L ,
and
.B \-L
on the command line replaces it. A shell that is neither interactive nor a
login shell ignores it. A restricted shell ignores it with a warning, as it
does the mood, because it would select the files run before the restrictions
take effect. KOSHCONF never carries it. Changing it with
.B koshconf set
in a running shell sources nothing. Use
.B set \-L
for that.
.PP
To keep the
.B kosh
mood for the session and still source ~/.bashrc, write
.PP
.RS
.EX
init_moods=bash
.EE
.RE
.PP
in the user file. Every mood sets KOSH and never exports it, so a line in
~/.bashrc guarded with
.B test \-z \(dq$KOSH\(dq
runs only in another shell, and a line guarded with
.B test \-n \(dq$KOSH\(dq
runs only in Koshka:
.PP
.RS
.EX
test \-z "$KOSH" && source /usr/share/bash\-completion/bash_completion
test \-n "$KOSH" && alias ll='ls \-l'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Listing bash also sets BASH_VERSION in that session, as
.B \-L bash
does, so a ~/.bashrc checking it will proceed.
.SH KOSHCONF
.B KOSHCONF
is a dynamic shell variable holding the current settings in a binary
form, encoded as standard base64 with padding. It is never exported by itself.
After
.BR "export KOSHCONF" ,
each command the shell starts receives the settings current when it starts,
not a copy taken at the export. Another shell can receive it through
.B koshconf load
.I value
or through a KOSHCONF variable in its environment.
.B koshconf save
prints the current form, so
.B KOSHCONF=$(koshconf save)
will start a kosh with the settings of the shell that ran it.
.B koshconf save \-\-persist
writes them to the user settings file instead. The variable is
applied after the settings files and removed before any command runs:
.PP
.RS
.EX
ssh host KOSHCONF="$KOSHCONF" kosh
.EE
.RE
.PP
The decoded bytes are a sequence of records, each an option id, a
value length, and the value. The id and the length are unsigned LEB128 varints:
seven bits per byte, least significant group first, with the high bit set on
every byte but the last. A boolean value is one byte, 0 or 1. An enumeration is
a varint holding the value's position in its list. A string is its UTF-8 bytes.
The ids are listed by
.BR kosh (1)
and never change meaning. Only the mood and the interactive options are
written. An interactive option affects only the interactive editor, the
history, or the presentation of diagnostics, never how a script runs. A child
shell loads the settings files itself, so semantic options such as
posix.interpreter.exit_on_command_failure, interpreter.mimic_shebang, and the debug
reports never pass through the environment. Bash exports SHELLOPTS only on
request.
.PP
A reader skips a record with an unknown id, an option it never carries, or a
value a settings file would reject, and reports a warning. Only the
canonical form is accepted. The base64 text must carry its padding, every
varint must use the fewest bytes, and the ids must strictly ascend in writer
order. A blob breaking any of these rules, or with truncated records,
is rejected as a whole with a warning identifying the broken rule.
.SH ERRORS
A missing settings file is skipped. An unreadable one, and a system file
failing the ownership check under FILES, produce a warning. A line
without an equals sign, an unknown option name, an invalid value, an unclosed
quote, text after a closing quote, and an option a file cannot hold each
produce a warning identifying the file and line, and the line is skipped.
The warning for an unknown option name suggests a close option name when one
exists. It renders a control byte or invalid UTF-8 byte from the file as an
escape in its message and as a question mark in the source excerpt. A settings
file never stops the shell.
.PP
Shell startup files produce the ordinary shell diagnostics described in
.BR kosh (1).
.SH MIGRATION
The files /etc/koshrc and ~/.koshrc are not read. An interactive shell in the
.B kosh
mood prints one warning about them when either exists. A non-interactive shell
and a shell started with
.B \-Q
stay silent. Move option settings into
.I kosh.conf
with
.B "koshconf set"
.I option value
.BR \-\-persist ,
and move shell code into ~/.bashrc, selected with
.BR mood=bash ,
.BR init_moods=bash ,
or
.B \-L bash
on the command line.
.SH EXAMPLES
A user file that keeps the bash grammar, edits with vi keys, and lets patterns
match dotfiles in every script:
.PP
.RS
.EX
mood=bash

editor.auto_close_brackets_and_quotes=on
completion.menu_style=plain

editor.history.max_entries=20000

editor.base_mode=vi

bash.interpreter.glob_includes_dotfiles=on
.EE
.RE
.PP
.B koshconf set
.I option value
.B \-\-persist
writes the same lines. In a file written by
.BR "koshconf create" ,
.B grep glob ~/.config/kosh/kosh.conf
will find every globbing setting.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kosh (1)
